// S~S  SimpleSal signal starts  S~S

// [Coexisting with ssUI.note]          search in ssTEA source files for a reference to this file.
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   This is one solution for a need to communicate data to events that act based on the data.  It is
   also possible to use a set of global variables per event; the named data is specific to each.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   These field names remap the generic AppEventVar fields into variable names used by the Application.
   The event code may refer to this "variable" and the ssHL-language compiler will remap it to the field name.
   The variable DriveValue will signal the actual value that should be written to the Mesa pin,
   values defined here for a digital pin are a function of what the receiver of the signal thinks.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   This typedef extends to each event structure used by ssTEA/ssUI to signal and manage an event.
   When an Event is granted agency, the Event descriptor includes a field of values set by the App.
   Because ssTEA includes this data structure declaration in the ssE event data structure,
   there must be a declaration of some form here to satisfy the compiler.  A single 1-byte variable
   will consume 1 byte for every event; should this be a problem, you could just #ifdef everywhere.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The benefit of alternative 2 is that the Application can continue as it was written before ssTEA.
   The benefit of alternative 1 is that each Event can refer to the fields as that Event needs to,
   so there doesn't need to be a super-declaration with all the fields used by any Event in the App.
   There are two sources to declare variables: borrow ssTEA's examples, or create your own examples.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
   Alternative 1 (borrow fields in the ssTEA declaration):
//
   The variables are generic, meaningless, can be used with the form "#define Useful Meaningless".
       #define DrivePinNow  a_boolean
       #define DriveCt      an_int
       #define DriveValue   Bits8_A
//
   Software that references the App use of the fields refers to the "Useful", in context of the App.
       if (pEventData->app_EventData.DrivePinNow)
       {
           Digitally_DrivePin (pEventData->app_EventData.DriveValue);
           pEventData->app_EventData.DriveCt++;
       }
   Users are of course free to insert a meaningless field here, and then use the useful name in code.
//
   Alternative 2 (create App-Event data in the Event-specific data):
//
   The App may declare the contents of the data structure using any types declared within the App
   by this point in the build process.  For example, below, "Sneak_pApiData" was added by the App;
   it tells an Event exactly which ssTEA API the Event is using, without a run-time search/lookup.
//
   Software referencing per-event data first retrieves the pointer to this App data for this Event.
        typedef struct ThisEvents_AppVars_s
        {
            boolean     AppVarDrivePinNow;
            int         AppDriveCt;
            Bits_8      AppVarDriveValue;
        }   ThisEvents_AppVars_t, *ThisEvents_pAppVars_t;
//
       Initialization of the pointer to App data within the Event "app_EventData" data structure.
            EventData.app_EventData.Sneak_pApiData = Create_Sneak_pApiData (ThisEvent);
//
        ThisEvents_pApiData = pEventData->app_Event_data.Sneak_pApiData;
        if (ThisEvents_pApiData->AppVarDrivePinNow)
        {
            Digitally_DrivePin (ThisEvents_pApiData->AppVarDriveValue);
            ThisEvents_pApiData->AppVarDriveCt++;
        }
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   ssTEA data types are declared here, so they exist and may be referenced in the App's Event Data
   built deep into the Event Descriptor provided to the Event when Agency is Granted by ssTEA.
   If the App Event Data does not include any ssTEA types, the include can be removed;
   the necessary inclusion of ssTEA data types will be covered by "ss_include_dcl.h".
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// S~S  SimpleSal signal ends  S~S
